  "prediction": "The report highlights the demographic trends in the technology workforce and the efforts of the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) and the Department of Labor's Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs (OFCCP) to oversee technology companies and contractors' compliance with equal employment opportunity and affirmative action requirements.\n\nThe technology sector has major employment hubs across the country, including the San Francisco Bay area, the greater New York City region, and the Washington-Arlington-Alexandria region. However, female, Black, and Hispanic workers comprise a smaller proportion of technology workers compared to their representation in the general workforce from 2005 through 2015.\n\nThe EEOC and OFCCP have implemented various measures to address diversity and inclusion in the technology sector. The EEOC has released reports analyzing EEO-1 data on diversity in the technology sector and has initiated an internal working group to identify practices to improve gender and racial diversity in technology. The OFCCP has conducted routine evaluations of selected federal contractors, including those in the technology sector, for compliance with federal nondiscrimination and affirmative action requirements.\n\nHowever, the report identifies several weaknesses in the EEOC and OFCCP's processes that impact the effectiveness of their efforts. The EEOC has not consistently captured information on industry codes, which impedes its ability to conduct industry sector analysis. The OFCCP faces delays in its compliance review process and has not analyzed its closed evaluations to understand the causes of these delays.\n\nThe report makes six recommendations to improve oversight of federal contractors, including requiring contractors to disaggregate demographic data for the purpose of setting placement goals in their affirmative action programs. The Department of Labor agrees with four of the five recommendations, while the EEOC has not agreed or disagreed with the recommendation to develop a timeline to complete the planned effort to clean IMS data for a one-year period.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for the EEOC and OFCCP to improve their processes and address the challenges facing the technology sector in terms of diversity and inclusion. By implementing these recommendations, the EEOC and OFCCP can better ensure that technology companies and contractors comply with equal employment opportunity and affirmative action requirements.",
